              deliberately without ROLE_REGISTRAR: a secret is a leaf

  register    0x85153573ed1dea94deaf62b35366c386c40940811da86dd2f3427af41a7cec00


1 · store — four records, one signature before them
---------------------------------------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs store vault alice "correct horse battery staple vault demo"

The ephemeral key is derived from a signature over a fixed message before
anything is written. It is not a transaction and approves nothing; it exists so
that the key can be reproduced later on any machine, from the wallet alone.

  nextkey.eph          k12R8orEcikSL06YdF1yp594ukFx7V/RVZS9XzmT3VM=
                       0x9d1057f9c77c8c77d12c96c42ce5ab05a52be51466502aa32dfb811cb3a58859
  nextkey.eph.sealed   wrapped to alice, so no signature is needed to open
                       0xcbec899aac3421eaaa0e58926730624cca2406f0876483766faf2f38c8bf573c
  nextkey.secret       39 characters, AES-256-GCM under a fresh key
                       0xce9d8ea43da7cb220e33832c832068685a15cdcaca0958d5a39b245d70f1f8e5
  nextkey.g2.4315cf86db9a23915248e0fd2c12b14d   the owner's own grant
                       0x622a636105e46f2cbefd732257a69b95d287c0964fbd4872a1e81b8774c15231

Note the fourth record's name. Under v1 it would have read
`nextkey.grant.<sha256 of alice's public key>` — computable by anyone holding
that public key, and therefore a public statement that alice has access. The
name above is derived from the shared secret between the ephemeral key and
alice's, so it says nothing to anyone who lacks one of the two private halves.


2 · eph — both recovery routes, compared against each other
------------------------------------------------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs eph vault alice

  scheme      v2
  nextkey.eph  k12R8orEcikSL06YdF1yp594ukFx7V/RVZS9XzmT3VM=
  nextkey.eph.sealed  172 characters
  recovery    ✓ via the sealed record
              both routes were available and agree

This is the line worth reading twice. The sealed record and the signature
derivation are independent of one another — one needs an identity key file, the
other a wallet — and they produced the same 32 bytes. Deterministic signing is
therefore not an assumption in this design; it is a measurement, taken here on a
real name and separately in scripts/probe-signing.mjs.


3 · open, as the owner
-----------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs open vault alice

  vault.nextkey.eth  opened as alice · v2
  correct horse battery staple vault demo


4 · share — a recipient who never registered with us
-----------------------------------------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs share vault alice anna.nextkey.eth

  their public key comes from their own ENS record
  ephemeral key recovered from nextkey.eph.sealed
  grant record  nextkey.g2.eb1914a579630175820344417822511f
                0x7c685dab0c612065d95c43ae7a6c96edaf22c3c9e2010b1f220947de136c0ff5

Two grants now sit on this name. Their record names share no visible structure
and neither can be derived from the other, from the ciphertext, or from any
public key. An observer sees two records with unrelated hexadecimal names.

  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs open vault anna
  vault.nextkey.eth  opened as anna · v2
  correct horse battery staple vault demo


5 · revoke — and the asymmetry it must produce
------------------------------------------------
  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs revoke vault anna.nextkey.eth alice

  ephemeral key recovered from nextkey.eph.sealed
  grant record  nextkey.g2.eb1914a579630175820344417822511f
  the ciphertext stays; the wrapped key is cleared
                0x8f655d6bd3b74e50c2ec696ab503f1843c54aba78465e336c68380ec1bd7fddf

The owner recomputed anna's record from anna's published key. No index of
outstanding grants exists or is needed: the same asymmetry that hides the
address from strangers hands it to the owner.

  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs open vault anna
  ✗  no grant for "anna" at vault.nextkey.eth — access was never given, or was revoked.
     (looked at nextkey.g2.eb1914a579630175820344417822511f, which only you and
     the owner can compute)

  node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs open vault alice
  vault.nextkey.eth  opened as alice · v2
  correct horse battery staple vault demo

One identity lost access; the other did not. That is the whole claim, and both
halves of it are needed — a revocation that took everyone's access with it would
be a different and much easier thing to build.


What this run does NOT show
---------------------------
Kept explicitly, because a log that only reports successes invites the reader to
assume the rest.

· **The browser half was not exercised here.** Every command above is the
  command-line tool. try.html writing v2 records to a visitor-owned name is
  tested in a real browser (web/test/playground.mjs, 27 checks, two languages)
  but has not been run against the chain under v2 as of this log. The v1
  equivalent was, and is recorded in evidence/playground-onchain.log.

· **No hardware wallet took part.** alice and anna are software identities. A
  Ledger recipient contributes the same X25519 shared secret through the device;
  under v2 it is asked to approve once rather than twice, because locating the
  record and unwrapping the key are the same ECDH. That property is implemented
  and unit-tested, not demonstrated on a device in this run.

· **Unlinkability is argued, not measured.** Nothing here proves an observer
  cannot correlate the two grants; what it shows is that their addresses are not
  functions of any public value. The supporting checks — that an adversary
  holding the name's ephemeral key *and* the recipient's public key reaches
  neither the address nor the plaintext — are in web/test/v2.mjs, and they are
  checks against our own construction, not a proof.

· **Revocation clears a record; it does not retract knowledge.** anna decrypted
  the secret in step 4. She still knows it. What ended is future access, and who
  may end it is enforced by the setter role on the name rather than by any
  server of ours.

· **This name is a subname of nextkey.eth**, inside our own registry. A
  second-level name outside it was exercised under v1 (nextkeydemo.eth) and is
  planned for v2.
